/* book Worked example: Q: A simply supported beam of 6 m effective span, b = 300 mm, d = 450 mm (M25, Fe415), has four 16 mm bars at midspan and carries a uniform factored load giving Mu = 110 kN·m at midspan. Two bars are to be curtailed and two continued to the supports. Find the theoretical and the actual cut-off points and check the shear condition, with two-legged 8 mm stirrups at 300 mm.
   1. Load: wu = 8 Mu/L² = 8 × 110/36 = 24.44 kN/m, and M(x) = 110 × 4x(6 − x)/36 at x m from a support.
   2. Four bars, 804.2 mm²: xu = 0.87 × 415 × 804.2/(0.36 × 25 × 300) = 107.5 mm; MR = 0.87 × 415 × 804.2 × (450 − 0.416 × 107.5) = 117.7 kN·m ≥ 110 kN·m.
   3. Two bars, 402.1 mm²: xu = 53.8 mm; MR = 0.87 × 415 × 402.1 × (450 − 0.416 × 53.8) = 62.1 kN·m.
   4. Theoretical cut-off: 110 × 4x(6 − x)/36 = 62.1 gives x² − 6x + 5.08 = 0, so x = (6 − √(36 − 20.32))/2 = 1.02 m from each support.
   5. Extension: the greater of d = 450 mm and 12φ = 192 mm, so 450 mm. Actual cut-off at 1.02 − 0.45 = 0.57 m from each support; the curtailed bars run from 0.57 m to 5.43 m.
   6. Development length: Ld = 645 mm. The curtailed bars are fully stressed at midspan and have 3.00 − 0.57 = 2.43 m beyond it, more than 645 mm. The continuing bars have 1.02 m plus their anchorage beyond the theoretical cut-off, also more than 645 mm.
   7. Shear at the actual cut-off: V = 24.44 × (3 − 0.57) = 59.4 kN. Capacity there: pt = 100 × 402.1/(300 × 450) = 0.298 %, τc = 0.36 + (0.298 − 0.25)/0.25 × 0.13 = 0.385 N/mm² (Table 19, M25), concrete 0.385 × 300 × 450 = 52.0 kN; stirrups 0.87 × 415 × 100.5 × 450/300 = 54.4 kN; total 106.4 kN.
   8. Two thirds of the capacity = 70.9 kN ≥ 59.4 kN, so the bars may stop in the tension zone with no extra stirrups.
   Answer: Theoretical cut-off 1.02 m from each support; actual cut-off 0.57 m (extension d = 450 mm). The shear there, 59.4 kN, is below two thirds of the capacity, 70.9 kN, so the curtailment is allowed. */
