/* book Worked example: Q: Detail the section of the edge beam designed for combined bending, shear and torsion: b = 300 mm, D = 600 mm, four 20 mm bottom bars, two 12 mm top bars, one 12 mm bar on each side face, 10 mm closed stirrups at 140 mm, moderate exposure, 20 mm aggregate. Check the cover, the clear spacing of the bottom bars, the effective depth and the side-face spacing.
   1. Moderate exposure: nominal cover 30 mm to the stirrups. Cover to the main bars = 30 + 10 = 40 mm.
   2. Width inside the stirrups: 300 − 2 × 30 − 2 × 10 = 220 mm.
   3. Clear spacing of the four 20 mm bars: (220 − 4 × 20)/3 = 140/3 = 46.7 mm.
   4. Minimum: greater of φ = 20 mm and 20 + 5 = 25 mm, so 25 mm. Maximum for Fe415: 180 mm. 46.7 mm lies between, so one layer works.
   5. Effective depth: d = 600 − 30 − 10 − 20/2 = 550 mm, as assumed in the design.
   6. Steel limits: 0.85 × 300 × 550/415 = 338 mm² ≤ 1257 mm² ≤ 0.04 × 300 × 600 = 7200 mm².
   7. Side faces: top bar centre 46 mm and bottom bar centre 50 mm from the faces; a side bar at mid-depth (300 mm) leaves gaps of 254 mm and 250 mm, below 300 mm.
   Answer: Cover 30 mm to the stirrups (40 mm to the main bars); clear spacing 46.7 mm (between 25 and 180 mm); d = 550 mm; steel 1257 mm² within 338 to 7200 mm²; side-face gaps 254 and 250 mm. The section is properly detailed. */
